!!Memphis!! (Egyptian Arabic: منف, romanized: //Manf//, pronounced
[[ [mænf\] | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEgyptian_Arabic ]];
Bohairic Coptic: ⲙⲉⲙϥⲓ; Greek: Μέμφις), or !!Men-nefer!!, was the ancient
capital of [[ Inebu-hedj ]], the first [[ nome | nome-egypt ]] of
[[ Lower Egypt ]] that was known as //mḥw// ("North").[[ ^2 ]] Its ruins are
located in the vicinity of the present-day village of !!Mit Rahina!! (Arabic:
ميت رهينة), in [[ Badrashin ]] markaz (county), [[ Giza Governorate ]],
Egypt.[[ ^3 ]]

Along with the [[ pyramid fields | memphite-necropolis ]] that stretch across
a desert plateau for more than 30 kilometres (19 mi) on its west, including
the famous [[ Pyramids of Giza | giza-pyramid-complex ]], Memphis and its
necropolis have been listed as a [[ World Heritage Site ]].[[ ^4 ]] The site
is open to the public as an [[ open-air museum ]].

According to legends related in the early third century BC by [[ Manetho ]], a
priest and historian who lived in the [[ Ptolemaic Kingdom ]] during the
[[ Hellenistic period ]] of Egyptian history, the city was founded by
[[ King | pharaoh ]] [[ Menes ]]. It was the
[[ capital | list-of-egyptian-capitals ]] of [[ ancient Egypt ]] (//Kemet// or
//Kumat//) during both the
[[ Early Dynastic Period | early-dynastic-period-of-egypt ]] and
[[ Old Kingdom ]] and remained an important city throughout
[[ ancient Egyptian history | history-of-ancient-egypt ]].[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]][[ ^7 ]]
It occupied a strategic position at the mouth of the [[ Nile Delta ]], and was
a hub of bustling activity. Its principal port, Peru-nefer (not to be confused
with [[ Peru-nefer ]] at [[ Avaris ]]), featured a high density of workshops,
factories, and warehouses that distributed food and merchandise throughout the
ancient kingdom. During its golden age, Memphis thrived as a regional centre
for commerce, trade, and religion.

Memphis was believed to be under the protection of the god [[ Ptah ]], the
patron of craftsmen. Its great [[ temple | egyptian-temple ]], //Hut-ka-Ptah//
(meaning "Enclosure of the [[ ka | egyptian-soul ]] of Ptah"), was one of the
most prominent structures in the city. The name of this temple, rendered in
Greek as //Aἴγυπτoς (Ai-gy-ptos)// by Manetho, is believed to be the
etymological origin of the modern English name //Egypt//.

The history of Memphis is closely linked to
[[ that of the region | history-of-ancient-egypt ]] itself. Its eventual
downfall is believed to have been due to the loss of its economic significance
in late antiquity, following the rise of coastal [[ Alexandria ]]. Its
religious significance was diminished after the abandonment of the
[[ ancient religion | ancient-egyptian-religion ]] following the
[[ Edict of Thessalonica ]] (380 AD), which made [[ Nicene Christianity ]] the
sole religion of the Roman Empire. By the
[[ Middle Ages | egypt-in-the-middle-ages ]], nearby Cairo had emerged as a
major political and economic center.

Today, the ruins of the former capital offer fragmented evidence of its past.
Many of its remains have become significant tourist destinations. The name
Memphis is also one of the names proposed for [[ The New Capital ]], naming it
after the ruined historical capital.

== Names

<tbody
  <tr
    <td
